  "failure_statement": "The agent declares work complete (or claims something is \"fixed\") without first reading and applying documented project policy that governs completion (e.g., a required changelog entry) and without running the declared verification check — repeating a pattern already flagged once before in this project.",
  "overlap_action": {
    "revises": "skill:$HOME/.claude/skills/fake-verify-before-claim/SKILL.md"
  },
  "remedy_content": {
    "body_markdown": "# Verify Before Claiming Completion\n\nBefore telling the user something is fixed, done, or complete:\n\n1. **Find the applicable policy.** Check for project-level completion requirements — CONTRIBUTING docs, CLAUDE.md rules, README sections, or explicit instructions in the current prompt/thread — that specify what \"done\" requires (e.g., a changelog entry, a passing test suite, a specific verification command).\n2. **Apply it, don't skip it.** If a changelog update, verification script, or other declared step is required, do it before claiming completion — not after the user objects.\n3. **Run the actual check.** If a verification command or test is declared for this kind of change, run it and report its real output. Never assert a fix worked without having executed the check.\n4. **Report plainly.** State what you verified and how (\"ran X, it passed\" / \"added the changelog entry per policy Y\"), not just \"done\" or \"fixed.\"\n\nIf you're unsure whether a documented policy applies, search for it (repo docs, CLAUDE.md, prompt instructions) before declaring completion rather than after being corrected.\n",
    "description": "Use when a completion claim requires verification — before reporting a task, fix, or release as done, check documented project policy for required completion steps (changelog entries, verification commands, sign-offs) and actually run/apply them first.",
    "name": "fake-verify-before-claim"
  },
  "remedy_type": "skill",
  "routing_rationale": "Incident 31 shows the agent declaring work \"fixed\"/complete before any verification check ran, and separately failing to read and apply a documented project policy (a required changelog entry) before that completion claim. Both symptoms are instances of the same failure mode already covered by R1's skill, fake-verify-before-claim (\"Use when a completion claim requires verification\") — that skill's scope already spans verification of completion claims generally, which includes checking documented project policy/requirements, not just running a literal verification command. R2's claude-md block is narrower (specifically: run the declared verification command for a release), so it doesn't fully cover the \"read documented policy before claiming done\" angle. Per merge bias, since R1 overlaps, this is a revision of R1 rather than a new sibling skill — I'm expanding the existing skill's body to explicitly include checking documented project policy (e.g., changelog requirements) as part of pre-completion verification, rather than creating a second skill that would compete for the same trigger.",
  "singleton": true
}
